<BaseLayout
  title="Philosophy | Emon Hossain — Practical AI Systems"
  description="Emon Hossain's working philosophy for practical AI systems: use deterministic automation for reliable tasks, AI for judgment-heavy work, and evidence before hype."
  ogTitle="Philosophy — Emon Hossain"
>
  <div class="container" style="padding:4rem 1.5rem;max-width:46rem;">
    <div class="eyebrow">PHILOSOPHY</div>
    <h1 style="font-size:2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--brand-ink);margin-top:1rem;">Use the least probabilistic system that can do the job well.</h1>

    <div style="color:var(--brand-stone-600);font-size:.92rem;line-height:1.85;margin-top:2rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.4rem;">
      <p>Many operational problems are not AI problems. They are process problems: unclear ownership, duplicated data entry, missing handoffs, inconsistent rules or information trapped in separate tools.</p>
      <p>I prefer <strong>deterministic automation</strong> for repeatable work such as routing, data movement, notifications and rule-based checks. I use <strong>probabilistic AI</strong> where language, interpretation, summarization, classification or flexible reasoning creates useful leverage.</p>
      <p>The goal is not to insert an LLM into every workflow. The goal is to make the workflow more reliable, easier to operate and easier to inspect when something goes wrong.</p>
      <p>AI systems also need boundaries. High-impact actions should have explicit permissions, review points, evidence and a clear owner. A system that cannot explain who is responsible for an exception is not finished.</p>
      <p>That leads to the public rule behind this brand: <strong>proof over hype</strong>. Show the build, the source, the limitation or the evidence before making the claim bigger.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
